Scalp Psoriasis (Mild Presentation)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Silvery scales at the anterior hairline, often misdiagnosed as seborrheic dermatitis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-041
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe scalp burning and bleeding secondary to scratching, requiring exclusion of secondary bacterial cellulitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate a painful, bleeding, and rapidly crusting rash on the scalp. Immediate clinical differentiation from an acute impetiginized infection, severe allergic contact dermatitis to hair products, or early herpes zoster was strictly required to determine the need for immediate antimicrobial therapy prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My scalp is on fire and I can't stop scratching it until it bleeds. I used a new shampoo and I'm terrified my hair is going to fall out from infection.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a chronic history of dandruff-like flaking with mild erythema. Over the last 24 hours, the patient experienced severe acute pruritus and burning on the anterior scalp, evolving into a continuous stinging sensation after vigorous mechanical scratching. The patient fears a severe, destructive infection or allergic reaction. Denies facial swelling, systemic fever, or purulent ocular discharge.
Functional Status: Independent, though highly distracted by intense scalp discomfort.
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, frequently reaching to scratch the scalp.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals well-demarcated erythematous plaques with thick silvery scales at the anterior hairline, extending slightly onto the forehead. The lesions are often misdiagnosed as seborrheic dermatitis. Multiple small, focal hemorrhagic crusts are present secondary to excoriation. There is no spreading, indurated erythema beyond the plaques, no fluctuance, and no honey-colored crusts. No cervical lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Assorted over-the-counter anti-dandruff and chemical shampoos visible in the bathroom; patient advised to cease use.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current systemic medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Fissuring and Pruritus in Scalp Psoriasis (Mild Presentation) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Bacterial Impetigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of characteristic weeping, honey-colored crusts, and no localized purulent discharge or regional lymphadenopathy.

Allergic Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct, hyperkeratotic silvery scale and sharply demarcated borders, rather than a diffuse, weeping eczematous reaction.

Herpes Zoster (Shingles) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral, symmetric plaque distribution along the hairline rather than a unilateral, dermatomal vesicular eruption.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acutely painful, bleeding scalp lesions with surrounding erythema, raising clinical suspicion for a rapidly spreading secondary bacterial infection or severe allergic reaction. Triage appropriately prioritized physical evaluation to prevent potential soft tissue infection and manage acute distress. Bedside assessment definitively excluded infectious complications, identifying the presentation as mechanical excoriation of chronic psoriatic plaques triggered by harsh topical products. Providing immediate clinical reassurance, ceasing the offending irritant, and averting an inappropriate emergency department presentation for intravenous antibiotics entirely satisfied the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed patient to immediately cease all harsh chemical shampoos. Prescribed a topical corticosteroid scalp lotion (e.g., betamethasone valerate 0.1%).
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply the prescribed steroid lotion directly to the red plaques tonight and do not wash your hair for the next 24 hours. By allowing the medication uninterrupted contact time with the skin, the deep inflammation will rapidly subside, stopping the burning sensation and the urge to scratch by morning.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 7-10 days to review response to topical steroids and discuss long-term maintenance therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if the redness rapidly spreads down your forehead or face, if the areas begin draining thick yellow pus, or if you develop a fever and chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive regarding the lack of microscopic infection; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Psoriasis causes the skin cells to turn over too rapidly, forming thick scales. When scratched, the small blood vessels bleed easily. It is not an infection and will respond well to targeted topical medication.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if the plaques become severely swollen, hot to the touch, or if you experience sudden, unexplained hair loss in the affected areas.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The pain and bleeding are from you scratching dry, inflamed skin, not a bacterial infection. The harsh shampoos you used stripped the skin of moisture and worsened the irritation. The steroid lotion will shrink the underlying inflammation.
Actionable Steps: Keep your fingernails trimmed very short to minimize damage when you feel the urge to rub the skin. Use only gentle, soap-free cleansers.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Scalp Psoriasis flare. No signs of bacterial infection or allergy. Stop harsh shampoos. Steroid scalp lotion prescribed. See GP in 7-10 days.

